# Line endings: LF everywhere, on every platform.
#
# Why this is not just style here: scripts/build-web.js inlines core.js, web-page.js and
# the seven JSON tables into dist/tradzh.html VERBATIM, and scripts/web-selftest.mjs then
# rebuilds that page and compares the two files BYTE FOR BYTE. core.autocrlf=true is the
# common Windows default, and a fresh clone that converts those sources to CRLF produces
# a page whose bytes differ from the committed one - so `npm test` fails on a clean
# checkout of a repo that is perfectly fine on the machine that built it.
#
# Diagnosed 2026-09 by cloning the repo into a temp directory and running the suite:
# the committed page had 0 CRLF, the rebuilt page had thousands.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git decide what is text, so a future binary file is untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exception, and it is a real one: batch files must be CRLF in the WORKING TREE.
# cmd.exe mis-parses bare LF (`goto` jumps to the wrong label, `set /p` reads an empty
# value), which is a rule this package states in SKILL.md and checks byte-wise in
# scripts/api-selftest.mjs. Without this line a committed .cmd would be checked out as
# LF - so the check would fail on a fresh clone of a repo whose author did nothing wrong.
# Git still stores LF internally (like every other text file here); only the checkout differs.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Belt and braces for the one generated file that is diffed by bytes: never rewrite it,
# whichever attribute rule would apply.
dist/tradzh.html -text
